doc:    "Found by soaking the `roundtrip` property after the two fixes above, mutating cli/tests/formatting/mixed_deeply_nested/input.buri, and minimised to the 163 bytes here. A match arm whose body is not already a block is printed as one of two candidates: the body beside the arrow, or the body in braces. The braced candidate wrote its own braces as a `{`, a `HardLine` and a `}` — braces that can only break — while the *next* read of the file finds a `Kind::Block` body, takes the other branch, and prints it through `block_doc`, whose braces are a group and can be flat. So an arm whose head is long enough to rule out the first candidate on its own, over a body that would have fitted beside the arrow, came out broken on one run and flat on the next, forever: a guard of sixteen chained fields over `\"\"` printed `=> {` / `\"\"` / `}` and then `=> { \"\" }`. Pre-existing, and verified so against the formatter as it stood before this wave. The same class as the two findings above and as the wrapped match guard before them — one shape emitted by a printer that the printer's own next read does not produce — and the fix is the same shape too: the braced candidate is now `block_doc(body)`, so both runs lay out one document. No existing formatting golden moved. The printed shape is pinned by cli/tests/formatting/match_a_wrapped_guard_keeps_its_arm_body_beside_the_arrow/."
property: "roundtrip"
status: FIXED
